---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Journey from '../components/Journey.astro';
import { AUTHOR, SITE } from '../config';
import { personSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const linkedin = AUTHOR.sameAs.find((u) => u.includes('linkedin'));

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'Readiness review',
    body: "I look at the four things that decide how hard this will be: your HR data, your Active Directory, your Entra ID tenant and licensing, and how joiners, movers and leavers are handled today. Most of the risk in these projects is in the HR data, so that's where I spend the most time.",
    outcome: 'You get a written set of findings, the gaps that need fixing before anything is built, a recommended design, and a fixed quote for the build.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Design',
    body: "Together we decide what drives access (which attributes, which roles), what people get automatically and what they have to request, when accounts are created and switched off, and how it all connects to ServiceNow or your other systems. Every decision is written down with the options we turned down and why.",
    outcome: 'A solution design you sign off before any build starts, so scope is settled up front.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Build',
    body: "I build the governance side in your tenant while your current process keeps running, and test it against your real HR data rather than tidy samples. Nothing changes for your users until we switch it on.",
    outcome: 'A working, tested pipeline, built quietly alongside what you have today.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Go-live',
    body: "We switch it on in stages, a group of sites or teams at a time, so managers aren't flooded with new-starter emails on day one and any surprises stay small.",
    outcome: 'Automated joiner, mover and leaver processes running in production.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Handover',
    body: "Your team gets as-built documentation, runbooks for the things that will go wrong, and time with me to walk through how it all fits together. If you want someone on call afterwards, we can agree that separately.",
    outcome: 'A team that can run and change it without me.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'ProfessionalService',
    name: `${AUTHOR.name}, identity governance consulting`,
    url: `${SITE.url}/work-with-me/`,
    areaServed: ['Australia', 'New Zealand'],
    founder: personSchema(),
    description:
      'Microsoft Entra ID Governance and HR-driven provisioning for organisations in Australia and New Zealand, including hybrid Active Directory environments.',
  },
];
---
<Base title="Work with me" description="Microsoft Entra ID Governance and HR-driven provisioning for Australian and New Zealand organisations: from the HR record to the right access, automatically." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Work with me</h1>
      <p>I help organisations in Australia and New Zealand give people the right access automatically, from the moment HR enters them to the day they leave, using Microsoft Entra ID Governance. That includes hybrid environments, where Active Directory is still in the picture and most of the hard problems are.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>You might recognise this</h2>
      <p>New starters wait days for their accounts, or arrive to find half their access missing. Someone in IT sets people up by copying a colleague who does a similar job, along with everything that colleague picked up over the years. Leavers keep access for weeks because nobody told IT, and when an auditor asks who approved a particular person's access, the answer lives in an old ticket, if anywhere.</p>
      <p>Usually the HR or payroll system already knows who starts, moves and leaves, and when. The trouble is getting that information to drive access reliably, and that's the work I do.</p>

      <h2>How a project runs</h2>
      <p>Every project follows the same path, and you can stop after any stage. Starting with the readiness review on its own is a sensible way to find out what you're dealing with before committing to more.</p>
    </div>

    <Journey steps={steps} />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Getting HR data into Entra ID</h2>
      <p>Entra ID has built-in connectors for a few HR systems. Many payroll systems aren't on that list, and even when there is a connector, payroll data is rarely clean enough to decide access on its own.</p>
      <p>Where that's the case, I bring in an HR integration platform that I've used in production, licensed through a partner. It sits between your HR system and Entra ID, and it handles the parts that are hardest to build and maintain yourself:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>connecting to HR and payroll systems that Entra ID has no connector for</li>
        <li>turning payroll data into something access can be based on, like job titles grouped into roles and location codes matched to real sites</li>
        <li>recognising the awkward cases, such as a starter entered after their first day or a termination entered late, so the right workflows still run</li>
        <li>treating a missing record as "do nothing" rather than "this person has left", so a failed export can't switch off half the company</li>
        <li>creating a separate admin account from the same HR record for people who need one</li>
        <li>keeping a full history of every change, for when audit asks</li>
      </ul>
      <p>It's optional. If your HR system already connects to Entra ID cleanly, we won't need it.</p>

      <h2>Recent work</h2>
      <p>Projects I've led or designed, with the names left out:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An industrial business operating across Australia and New Zealand: payroll-driven provisioning into hybrid Active Directory and Entra ID, joiner, mover and leaver workflows, rule-based access packages, and ServiceNow integration.</li>
        <li>An Australian mutual bank: identity governance and privileged access together, from HR-driven provisioning through to BeyondTrust.</li>
        <li>A university and a healthcare distributor: privileged access management with BeyondTrust.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <a href="/articles/">articles</a> on this site come out of that work, if you'd like to see how I think about these problems before we talk.</p>

      <h2>Start with a conversation</h2>
      <p>Send me a message {linkedin ? <a href={linkedin} rel="me">on LinkedIn</a> : 'on LinkedIn'} with a line or two about your environment: which HR or payroll system you use, whether you still run Active Directory, and what's prompting you to look at this now.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
